# Rotation for every PathQuote log on the VPS: the backups, the nightly ACT!
# sync, the 15-minute ACT! drain, the Monday report and the alert unit
# (scripts/ops/*.service, each StandardOutput=append:/var/log/pq-<name>.log).
#
# Installed as /etc/logrotate.d/pathquote (docs/runbook.md, the write-back
# section, "Log rotation"); Ubuntu's logrotate.timer runs it daily. Check it
# with:  logrotate -d /etc/logrotate.d/pathquote
#
# No copytruncate, and no postrotate either: nothing holds these files open
# between runs. Every unit is Type=oneshot, and systemd opens an append:
# file afresh each time it starts the unit -- by its path, O_APPEND, created
# if missing -- for that run's processes, which let go of it when they exit.
# So once logrotate has renamed a log, the next run writes a new file at the
# old name. A run that is going while logrotate renames the file finishes in
# the renamed one (pq-act-drain.log.1, say); delaycompress leaves that file
# uncompressed until the rotation after, so the run's last lines are never
# compressed under it, and are still there to read. Any unit can be mid-run
# when logrotate runs; the drain, every 15 minutes, is the likeliest.
#
# The one cost is the failure email (pq-backup-alert.sh), which tails 40
# lines of the log by its name. A run that started after the rotation is
# wholly in the new file, and the email carries it as before. A run that
# started before it and failed after it wrote its lines to the .1 file, so
# the email shows the new file's few lines instead; that run is in
# /var/log/<the unit's log>.1.
#
# Monthly, or sooner once a log passes 10M -- maxsize is checked on every
# daily run, so the drain's log, written 96 times a day, rotates when it is
# big rather than waiting out the month. Twelve kept: a year of them, for a
# log that stays under 10M a month.

/var/log/pq-*.log {
    monthly
    maxsize 10M
    rotate 12
    compress
    delaycompress
    missingok
    notifempty
}
